{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>דליו</b> — from <b>כי דליתני</b> (for You have drawn me up), whose meaning is \"You have lifted me up,\" and it is deficient [i.e., shortened] the letter heh of the infinitive, meaning <b>הדליו</b> (the lifting up) — \"he lifts up the legs\" of a lame man, who has no legs, meaning: a proverb without sense is like a body without legs. Another interpretation: <b>דליו</b> — like <b>דללו</b> (he weakened it), and the yod of <b>דליו</b> is like the alef in <b>זאו</b> נהרים (the flowing of rivers), meaning: he weakened the legs of the lame one, without legs, and so too a proverb without sense."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The verse under discussion is Proverbs 26:7, דליו שוקים פסח משל. The phrase 'ה letter of the infinitive' refers to the known infinitive form הדלות; Ibn Ezra calls it התימה (the feminine/definite article form). The reference to אלף in זאו נהרים alludes to the alef of לשון הקודש forms where a root-middle weak letter substitutes."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:24:52.740788586Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}